/// The smaller of two integers.
export fn min(value: Int, other: Int) -> Int {
  if value < other {
    value
  } else {
    other
  }
}

/// The larger of two integers.
export fn max(value: Int, other: Int) -> Int {
  if value > other {
    value
  } else {
    other
  }
}

/// This value, with its sign removed.
///
/// Unlike `min` and `max` beside it, this one can fail: the least `Int` has
/// no positive counterpart, and it traps there exactly as the builtin did.
/// It is the first fallible function in the standard library. A trap raised
/// here names its caller ([issue #258](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/258))
/// and, since ADR 0058, is blamed on it: the caller's line is the diagnostic's
/// primary location and this file's line is the context shown beside it.
export fn abs(value: Int) -> Int {
  if value < 0 {
    -value
  } else {
    value
  }
}

/// Appends the decimal text of `value` to `buffer`: what `"{value}"` shows.
///
/// Not exported, and nothing a program can name: the lowering calls it for
/// every `Int` piece of an interpolation, into the buffer that string is being
/// assembled in (#403). It replaced the Rust intrinsic `Int.renderInto`.
///
/// # One digit a push, most significant first
///
/// The buffer only appends, so the leading digit has to be known before any is
/// written: `power` is found as the largest power of ten the magnitude reaches,
/// and each digit is the quotient by it as it is divided back down to one.
/// Each digit is one byte append — ADR 0062's length read, ensure of one, byte
/// store at that length and commit of one, which both tiers run as one step and
/// answer in place when the buffer has room (the interpolation sized it for a
/// whole `Int`) — so a digit costs a division, a multiplication and a store,
/// and no call.
///
/// The append is written out rather than called as `std.stringbuilder`'s
/// `appendByteInto`, which is the same four statements: one standard-library
/// module cannot name another's private function. The byte is bound first, so
/// that its arithmetic is not inside the window.
///
/// # Nothing here can overflow
///
/// Everything happens on the non-positive side of zero, because every `Int`
/// has a non-positive counterpart and the least `Int` has none above zero. So
/// `rest` is `value` or its negation and never above zero, `power` is minus a
/// power of ten, and a digit is the quotient of two non-positive numbers:
///
/// - `power` is multiplied by ten only while `rest / 10 <= power`. `rest / 10`
///   truncates toward zero, so ten times it is at least `rest`, and so is ten
///   times `power`: the product is never formed where it would not fit;
/// - `digit * power` is at least `rest`, so the subtraction stays in range;
/// - no divisor is zero, and `power` is `-1` only for the last digit, when
///   `rest` is above `-10`, so no quotient overflows.
///
/// It must never interpolate an `Int` itself, which would call itself.
fn renderInto(value: Int, buffer: ByteBuffer) {
  var rest = value
  if value < 0 {
    let minus = '-'
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
    core.bytesStore(buffer, at, minus)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
  } else {
    rest = -value
  }
  let tenth = rest / 10
  var power = -1
  while tenth <= power {
    power = power * 10
  }
  while power < 0 {
    let digit = rest / power
    let byte = '0' + digit
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
    core.bytesStore(buffer, at, byte)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
    rest = rest - digit * power
    power = power / 10
  }
  // The answer, written where the call wants it. Left to the loop, it is a
  // `()` made aside and copied, which every expanded call site would keep.
  ()
}
